  "markup": "\u003Cp\u003EPharmacists can help improve heart failure care by identifying treatment gaps, medication-related harm and barriers to adherence. A complete review should cover prescribed, over-the-counter and complementary medicines, as well as recent hospital or specialist medication changes.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Ch2\u003EMedicines That May Worsen Heart Failure\u003C/h2\u003E\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003ENSAIDs, including COX-2 inhibitors, which can cause fluid retention and renal impairment\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003EVerapamil and diltiazem in people with HFrEF\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003EThiazolidinediones, such as pioglitazone\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003ESaxagliptin and alogliptin, which require caution\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003ECertain antiarrhythmic medicines\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003EHigh-sodium effervescent or soluble preparations\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003Ch2\u003EKey Safety Checks\u003C/h2\u003E\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003EAvoid combining an ACE inhibitor with sacubitril/valsartan, and ensure a 36-hour washout when switching between them.\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003EMonitor potassium and renal function when using ACE inhibitors, angiotensin receptor blockers, ARNIs or mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists.\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003ECheck for added hyperkalaemia risk from potassium supplements, potassium-based salt substitutes and trimethoprim.\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003EWatch for signs of digoxin toxicity, including nausea, visual disturbance, bradycardia and confusion, particularly when renal function declines.\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003ECounsel people taking SGLT2 inhibitors about genital fungal infections, volume depletion and ketoacidosis, which can occur with near-normal blood glucose.\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003Ch2\u003EPatient Counselling and Communication\u003C/h2\u003E\u003Cp\u003EPharmacists should also explain why each medicine is used and which side effects to report. Patients should know to seek advice early if they become dehydrated through vomiting, diarrhoea or poor intake, as some medicines may need temporary adjustment. Dose administration aids and simplified regimens may support adherence. Any concerns or recommendations should be communicated clearly to the prescriber and care team.\u003C/p\u003E"
}]]></Value>
    </bodyContent>
